thousands of people cross it every day  it's the heart of the ukrainian capital  kiev independence square  people often call it just the maidan the  square  has been coming here for more than a  year he's actually from the provinces  but since the beginning of the  revolution in late 2013 the maidan has  been his home  for months there were demonstrations on  the maidan against the autocratic regime  of the pro-russian president viktor  yanukovych  months of protests for democratic reform  since then the maidan has symbolized  that revolution  maxim balenko and his friends were there  from the very start  the square has changed his life  i've understood that my opinion bears  weight i'm worth something i can change  something  nowadays many of the demonstrators of  old crossing the square are soldiers  fighting in eastern ukraine against the  pro-russian separatists  it's at the heart of the capital  and it's now also part of the front in a  way  maxime and his friends are converting a  house here into a hostel for soldiers  passing through  it's symbolic it's an extension of the  protests on the maidan  the war is a major effort for the whole  of ukraine  without the thousands of volunteers it  said the government troops wouldn't have  a chance  after 45 days the soldiers come back  from the east  many stop off here  and can spend the night for free  we're the first to offer them help when  they come to kiev with a warm shower  many want to wash their clothes we give  them civilian clothing if we have it  the one-time revolutionaries are now  hostile staff  the nine friends are renovating more and  more rooms as long as the building  supplies last  even during the protest the most  important thing was being able to  organize yourself we all played a part  and we all understood where our place  was here  for ukrainians the maidan is more than  just the central square in the capital  kiev  it symbolizes the revolution of dignity  as it's called here the revolution that  took place more than a year ago  and it's the central meeting place every  day  and it has other attractions costumed  comic book figures who let you take  their pictures for a tip  pictures on display photographs of the  revolution  they're a reminder of the months of  struggle for democracy in icy winter  temperatures  the first of a hundred victims died  nearby in front of the national library  sergey oliver was in the library from  the very beginning  he and some colleagues set up an  infirmary for the wounded there  surgery was performed on book tables  i hope it brings us some change for the  better  that's what they all hope here on the  maidan and in the entire war-torn  country
 

